      Suppose k ≥ 2d are positive integers. A (k, d)-coloring of an undirected graph
G is a mapping f : V (G) → {0, 1, . . . , k − 1} such that for any edge xy of G,
d ≤ |f (x) − f (y)| ≤ k − d. If G has a (k, d)-coloring then we say G is (k, d)-colorable.
The circular chromatic number χc (G) of G is defined as

                        χc (G) = inf{k/d : G is (k, d)-colorable }.

In fact, to determine the circular chromatic number of G, it suffices to check finitely
many k, d whether G is (k, d)-colorable. In [22, 23, 24] we see that

