      Let ω be an acyclic orientation of a connected undirected simple graph G. We
denote by (G, ω) the corresponding oriented graph. Sometimes, by abuse of notation,
we will simply write ω instead of (G, ω). A vertex in (G, ω) with zero outdegree (resp.,
indegree) is called a sink (resp., source). Let sink(ω) (resp., source(ω)) denote the set
of sinks (resp., sources) in (G, ω). One can obtain a sequence of acyclic orientations
ω0 , ω1 , ω2 , · · · from ω in such a way that ω0 = ω, and ωi (i ≥ 1) is obtained from ωi−1
by reversing the orientations of the edges incident to the sinks of wi−1 . We say that
{ωi }∞
     i=0 is generated by ω. In Figure 1 we depict a sequence of acyclic orientations

{ωi }∞
     i=0 on C5 which is generated by ω0 . Note that this sequence has the periodic

property: ωi = ωi+5 for any i ≥ 0.



                                                w  1


                         w0                                               w2


