        Figure 1: A sequence of acyclic orientations {ωi }∞
                                                          i=0 generated by ω0


      Obviously the sequence {ωi }∞
                                  i=0 generated by an acyclic orientation ω has the

following periodic behavior: There exist positive integers p and M such that ωi = ωi+p
for every i ≥ M. The value p at its minimum is denoted by pω and is called the period
of ω. The pω acyclic orientations ωM , ωM +1 , · · · , ωM +pω −1 is called a period generated
by ω. For any k ≥ M and ℓ ≤ pω − 1, the sequence

                        h|sink(ωk )|,|sink(ωk+1 )|,· · ·,|sink(ωk+ℓ )|i

is called a sub-pattern of a period generated by ω, sometimes we call it a sub-pattern of
ω for short. Let muω denote the number of times that the vertex u becomes a sink in
the period generated by ω. It was shown in [5] that muω = mvω for any two vertices u
and v. So we write mω instead of muω , and mω is called the multiplicity of ω. Clearly,
the following result, shown in [5, 21], is a special case of Theorem 6.

                                              11
Corollary 7 Let G be a connected simple graph. Then
